LESSONS_S4 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Sales Order: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Sales Order in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Sales Order field walk; state what submission commits and reserves; plan per-row delivery schedules including multi-wave orders; operate the amendment and cancellation discipline; configure payment schedules and handle advances; and run the order book reads.</p><p><b>What a Sales Order is.</b> The Sales Order is the agreement: the business commits to supply these items, in these quantities, at these prices, on this schedule, on these payment terms. Everything before it was offer (module 3); everything after it executes it — the Delivery Notes ship against it (module 5), the invoices bill against it (module 7), the stock reserves for it, and the credit exposure counts it. The order is therefore the document the business is held to, and this module's disciplines exist because commitments are expensive to state wrongly.</p><p><b>Order sources.</b></p><p>1. <b>Conversion from a quotation</b> — the enterprise and negotiated lane (module 3, Chapter 7): the order inherits the final accepted version with its evidence.<br>2. <b>Direct entry</b> — the routine trade lane: standing customers ordering from the lists, entered against the registers with no quotation required per the policy. Direct orders carry the same disciplines — pre-filled rates, the units read, credit checked — with the customer's purchase order reference attached where one exists.</p><p><b>What submission does.</b> Stated here and detailed in Chapter 3: the order's quantities enter the reserved figures the stock system carries; the credit check runs against the customer's limit; the delivery and billing machinery gains its basis; and the order joins the order book the business plans from. An unsubmitted draft commits nothing; a submitted order commits everything it states.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The sales function enters and maintains orders; commercial changes re-enter the deviation ladder (module 3's bands apply to order amendments exactly as to offers); schedule changes follow the amendment procedure; and the standing rule: <b>the order states the whole agreement — nothing is promised beside it, and nothing on it changes without its documented amendment.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the field walk. Chapter 3: the commitment mechanics. Chapter 4: the delivery schedule. Chapter 5: amendments and cancellations. Chapter 6: the payment schedule and advances. Chapter 7: the order book reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Sales Order doctype", "12", "<p>Open Sales Order (awesome bar) &gt; New — or arrive here by conversion from a quotation. Complete or verify the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Customer and the header pre-fill.</b> The customer selection pulls module 1's machinery: currency, price list, payment terms template, addresses, contact. On converted orders the quotation's values carry; the verification rule stands either way — the header is read before the rows.</p><p><b>2. Customer's Purchase Order.</b> The customer's own PO number and date, entered where the customer issues one (enterprise and institutional customers almost always do). The reference prints on the delivery and invoice documents, and the customer's payables function matches against it — a missing PO reference is a payment delay at the customer's end.</p><p><b>3. Delivery Date.</b> The header delivery date, and — the field that matters for real schedules — the <b>per-row delivery dates</b> in the items table: each row carries its own date, which is how one order states a multi-wave schedule (Chapter 4). The header date is the latest commitment; the rows state the plan.</p><p><b>4. The items table.</b> Per row: item, quantity, UOM (the unit column read, per module 2's discipline), rate (inherited from the quotation or pre-filled from the lists; deviations per the ladder), amount, the per-row delivery date, and the source warehouse where the site sets it at order time. Converted orders are checked row-by-row against the final quotation version — the module 3 handoff's receiving side.</p><p><b>5. Taxes and Charges.</b> Per the template, as quoted. The order's tax total is the invoice's tax total; discrepancies trace to template or classification errors and are fixed at the source, not edited on the document.</p><p><b>6. Payment Terms Schedule.</b> The terms template generates the schedule table — due dates and portions (Chapter 6). Milestone structures (the advance-delivery-completion split) are entered here as the schedule the invoices will follow.</p><p><b>7. Terms, source, and attachments.</b> The T&C as quoted; the source quotation linked (conversion does this); the acceptance evidence attached (module 3, Chapter 7) — the customer's PO document, the signed offer. The order file is the agreement file.</p><p><b>8. Save, review, submit.</b> Draft review per the value threshold (the preparer/reviewer split on commitments above the band); submission runs the credit check and commits the order (Chapter 3).</p><p><b>Verification per order.</b> Header read; rows against the accepted version or the lists; units read; per-row dates stating the real plan; the schedule per the agreed terms; evidence attached; then submit — and the order confirmation to the customer per Chapter 3.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The commitment mechanics", "12", "<p>Submission turns a draft into a commitment. This chapter is exactly what commits, where it shows, and the confirmation that closes the loop.</p><p><b>1. Stock reservation.</b> Each submitted row's undelivered quantity enters the <b>Reserved Qty</b> on the item-warehouse Bin (the Inventory track, module 1, Chapter 6). The consequences:</p><p>1. <b>Projected quantity falls</b> — replenishment arithmetic sees the commitment: the reorder machinery orders against promised stock, not just shelf stock.<br>2. <b>Availability questions answer honestly</b> — the next order's preparer sees projected, not actual, and does not promise the same stock twice.<br>3. <b>Reservation hygiene matters</b> — the stale-order reservations the Inventory track's monthly read hunts (orders that will never fulfil, holding stock on paper) are this module's Chapter 7 hygiene, read from the selling side.</p><p><b>2. The credit check.</b> Submission tests outstanding exposure plus this order against the customer's limit (module 1, Chapter 5): the block posture refuses the over-limit order; the named bypass role may pass it as a listed decision. The order is where credit exposure is created — the invoice merely documents what the order committed — which is why the check runs here and not only at invoicing.</p><p><b>3. The execution basis.</b> The submitted order becomes the reference every downstream document draws from:</p><p>1. <b>Delivery Notes</b> create from the order, drawing rows and quantities (module 5) — the %delivered figure tracks fulfilment per row.<br>2. <b>Sales Invoices</b> bill against the order per the payment schedule (module 7) — the %billed figure tracks it.<br>3. <b>The two percentages together</b> state the order's life at a glance: delivered and billed to completion is a closed commitment; the gaps between them are Chapter 7's reads.</p><p><b>4. The order confirmation.</b> After submission, the confirmation goes to the customer per the policy: the order document (the site's print format) stating what was agreed — items, quantities, prices, schedule, terms. The confirmation is the last cheap moment to catch a disagreement: the customer reading their own commitment back finds the wrong quantity or date now, against a document, rather than at the delivery dock against a truck. Enterprise orders confirm as standing procedure; routine trade confirms per the policy's threshold.</p><p><b>5. What submission does not do.</b> It does not move stock (deliveries do), does not create receivables (invoices do), and does not collect the advance (the payment entry does, Chapter 6). The order is the promise; the downstream documents are the performance — and keeping the two distinct is what lets the reads in Chapter 7 say precisely where any order stands.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The delivery schedule", "12", "<p>Real orders ship in waves. This chapter is the scheduling machinery and its planning reads.</p><p><b>1. Per-row delivery dates.</b> The items table carries a delivery date per row — the mechanism for every schedule shape:</p><p>1. <b>The single-wave order</b> — all rows on one date; the header date matches.<br>2. <b>The multi-wave order</b> — the same item split across rows with different dates (100 units on the first date, 100 on the second), or different items on different dates per the plan. The rows state the schedule; the header carries the final date.<br>3. <b>The rule:</b> the rows state the <b>real</b> plan agreed with the customer, not a placeholder — every planning read below computes from these dates, and a schedule entered as one optimistic date produces overdue flags on stock the customer never expected yet.</p><p><b>2. Scheduling against stock.</b> The order's dates are checked against availability at entry: projected quantity per item per warehouse (the Inventory track's figure) against each wave's quantity and date, with incoming purchase schedules visible where the site exposes them. A wave the stock cannot meet is a conversation at order time — the date moved, the quantity split, or the replenishment expedited — not a discovery at picking. For orders that drive procurement (the enterprise order requiring equipment purchase), the procurement lane is triggered from the order per the site's flow, and the wave dates account for the supplier's lead time.</p><p><b>3. Wave execution.</b> Each wave ships as its own Delivery Note against the order (module 5's territory): the note draws the wave's rows and quantities, the %delivered figure advances, and the remaining rows hold their dates. The order remains the single reference across all waves — one agreement, several performances, each documented.</p><p><b>4. The delivery planning read — weekly.</b> Open order rows by delivery date:</p><p>1. <b>The coming window</b> — rows due in the planning horizon, grouped by warehouse and route: the picking and despatch workload, visible a week out.<br>2. <b>The overdue rows</b> — rows past their date and undelivered: each is a commitment the business is currently missing, chased to its cause (stock, transport, a customer hold) and either delivered or re-dated by documented amendment with the customer's agreement (Chapter 5). An overdue row left unexplained is the read failing at its purpose.<br>3. <b>The at-risk rows</b> — due rows whose projected stock is short: flagged before their date, while the expedite-or-re-date conversation is still cheap.</p><p><b>5. Schedule changes.</b> Dates move — customers defer, stock delays. Every date movement is an amendment per Chapter 5's procedure: the row's date updated through the amendment lane with the reason and the customer's agreement noted. The schedule's history then reads honestly: what was promised, what moved, why, and with whose agreement — which is the record the service-level conversation at year-end is conducted from.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Amendments and cancellations", "12", "<p>Agreements change; the record must change correctly. This chapter is the discipline.</p><p><b>1. The change taxonomy.</b> Changes route by what they touch:</p><p>1. <b>Commercial changes</b> — rates, discounts, added lines at negotiated prices: these re-enter module 3's deviation ladder at the band the change belongs to. An order amendment is not a side door around the offer's approvals; the concession that was never approved does not become approved by appearing on an amendment.<br>2. <b>Quantity changes</b> — increases run the credit and availability checks again (more commitment); decreases release reservation and are checked against work already done (a wave already picked or shipped does not un-ship by amendment — the return lane in module 5 handles physical reversals).<br>3. <b>Schedule changes</b> — date movements per Chapter 4.5: documented, reasoned, agreed.<br>4. <b>Administrative changes</b> — references, contacts, addresses for undelivered waves: ordinary edits with notes.</p><p><b>2. The amendment mechanics.</b> Submitted orders change through the site's controlled lanes:</p><p>1. <b>The update facility for submitted orders</b> — quantity and schedule updates on undelivered rows, permitted to the roles the matrix names, each update logged by the system. This is the standard lane for the common cases.<br>2. <b>Cancel-and-amend</b> — the full amendment for structural changes: the order cancels with its reason and the amended version issues carrying the reference, per the standing document rules. Downstream documents already submitted against the order gate what cancellation is possible — an order with deliveries posted amends around its performed portion.<br>3. <b>Either lane, the same records rule:</b> the change, the reason, the approver per the taxonomy, and the customer's agreement where the agreement moved — on the document trail, not in a phone call's memory.</p><p><b>3. Cancellations.</b> The order that will not proceed cancels with its reason code (customer cancelled, credit refused, superseded), releasing its reservations and closing its schedule rows. Cancellation reasons are a small defined set, per the standing reason-code pattern, because the quarterly cancellations read aggregates them into a why. Partial situations — some waves shipped, the remainder cancelled — close the undelivered rows per the procedure, leaving the performed portion intact and billed.</p><p><b>4. The status vocabulary.</b> The order's status states its life: To Deliver and Bill, To Bill, To Deliver, Completed, Cancelled, Closed. <b>Closed</b> is the deliberate early end: the customer takes no more against this order, remaining quantities release, the record preserves what happened. Closing is a decision with a reason, used at the stale-order hygiene read (Chapter 7) — the alternative, orders left open forever at partial delivery, is the misstated order book Chapter 9 lists among the errors.</p><p><b>5. The amendments read — monthly.</b> Orders amended in the month: the change, the lane, the approver, reconciled against the taxonomy's requirements — commercial changes carrying ladder approvals, schedule changes carrying agreements. The sibling of every fields-move-by-decision read in the programme, applied to commitments.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The payment schedule and advances", "12", "<p>The order states when money is due, and enterprise orders collect part of it before work starts. This chapter is the machinery.</p><p><b>1. The payment schedule table.</b> The order's Payment Terms section: the template (module 1, Chapter 5) generates the schedule rows — each a portion and a due basis. Structures the schedule expresses:</p><p>1. <b>The standing terms case</b> — one row: 100% due at the term's interval from invoice.<br>2. <b>The milestone case</b> — the enterprise pattern: an advance portion due at order, a portion due on delivery, a retention portion due at completion (the 50/40/10 shape). Each row's portion and timing enter as the negotiated agreement states, and the invoices (module 7) follow the schedule as their billing plan.</p><p><b>2. Advances against orders.</b> The advance portion collects before delivery:</p><p>1. <b>The request</b> — the payment request against the order for the advance portion, sent per the policy (the enterprise order does not enter the work queue until the advance clears, where the terms say so — the schedule is enforced, not decorative).<br>2. <b>The receipt</b> — the Payment Entry recording the customer's payment <b>against the Sales Order as an advance</b>: the entry references the order, the money lands in the bank account, and the customer's balance shows the advance held.<br>3. <b>The allocation</b> — when the invoices raise (module 7), the advance allocates against them per the schedule: the advance held becomes payment applied, and the customer's statement reads coherently — order, advance, invoice, allocation. An advance received but never allocated is a reconciliation item the receivables read surfaces; the allocation step is part of the invoicing procedure, not an afterthought.</p><p><b>3. Custody and the accounting boundary.</b> The sales function agrees the schedule (within the terms the ladder approved) and tracks the milestones; the accounts function receives, records, and allocates the money per the Accounts track's procedures. The boundary rule: <b>sales confirms what is due when; accounts confirms what arrived and where it applied</b> — and the order's schedule is the shared reference both read from.</p><p><b>4. The schedule reads.</b></p><p>1. <b>The advance status read — weekly on the enterprise book:</b> orders whose schedule requires an advance, against advances received — the unfunded order that entered the work queue is the finding.<br>2. <b>The milestone read — monthly:</b> due milestones against invoicing and collection: the delivered wave whose delivery-portion invoice never raised, the completed order whose retention was never billed. Both are revenue sitting unbilled through process gaps, and module 7's invoicing discipline consumes this read.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The order book reads", "12", "<p>The open orders are the business's committed future. This chapter is the reads that keep the book true and worked.</p><p><b>1. The order book — weekly.</b> All open orders with value, age, %delivered, %billed, and next scheduled date. The book is the selling function's working list and the owner's forward view: committed revenue, its schedule, and its execution state in one report. The weekly pass works it top-down by value: the largest commitments' next actions confirmed on calendar.</p><p><b>2. The overdue-delivery read — weekly (Chapter 4's read, restated as the book's column).</b> Rows past date, each with its cause and its action — delivered, expedited, or re-dated by documented amendment. The read's output is a short list with owners, not a long list with sighs.</p><p><b>3. The delivered-not-billed read — weekly.</b> Orders (and waves) where %delivered leads %billed beyond the invoicing cadence: performance the business has given away without yet asking for its money. Module 7 owns the invoicing procedure; this read is the order-side alarm that feeds it, and its target state is a list that clears at each invoicing run.</p><p><b>4. The billed-not-delivered read — monthly.</b> The inverse gap: invoicing ahead of delivery beyond the advance structure the schedule authorises. Legitimate cases trace to their schedule rows (the advance invoice per the milestone plan); the remainder are errors to correct before they become customer disputes.</p><p><b>5. The stale-order hygiene read — monthly.</b> Open orders with no movement (no delivery, no invoice, no amendment) across the policy's window:</p><p>1. <b>The reservation cost stated</b> — each stale order holds Reserved Qty against stock other orders could use (Chapter 3; the Inventory track reads the same figure from the other side).<br>2. <b>The action per order</b> — revived with a real schedule (the customer confirms new dates by amendment), or Closed with its reason, releasing the reservation.<br>3. <b>The target</b> — a book containing only live commitments, because every planning figure downstream (projected stock, committed revenue, the delivery workload) computes from it.</p><p><b>6. The cancellations and closures read — quarterly.</b> The quarter's cancelled and closed orders by reason code: the distribution goes to the owner as data (credit refusals rising, customer cancellations concentrated in a segment, closures from stock failure), with the orders named, per the standing rule.</p><p><b>7. The filing.</b> The book and its reads file with the sales records; the order file per order — the agreement, evidence, amendments, waves, schedule — remains the one-lookup answer for any commitment's history, and module 7 will add the invoicing and collection layers onto the same file.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the Montaigne AH order", "13", "<p>This chapter runs Chapters 2-7 through the order that module 3's negotiation produced. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — creation from the accepted version (Chapter 2).</b> The order creates from round two — the final accepted quotation — and is checked row-by-row against it: the 12-branch equipment lines with the <b>4% terminal concession intact</b> (terminals only, per the approval's named scope; scanners and printers at list), installation and training as restructured in round two. Montaigne AH's purchase order number enters the header; the acceptance document attaches. The header is read; the rows are read; the units are read.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the schedule entered (Chapters 2, 4, 6).</b></p><p>1. <b>Delivery:</b> the rollout plan enters as per-row dates in <b>three waves</b> — the first branches in the first wave, the remainder across the following two, each row carrying its wave's date; the header carries the final date.<br>2. <b>Payment:</b> the negotiated milestone structure enters as the schedule table — <b>50% advance at order, 40% on delivery, 10% at completion</b> — the rows stating portion and basis exactly as the agreement reads.</p><p><b>Step 3 — one change at signature (Chapter 5).</b> Montaigne AH requests one change as they sign: <b>the training component moves</b> to sit after the second delivery wave rather than the first. The change is classified per the taxonomy: a schedule change with no commercial movement — the training row's date amends through the standard lane with the reason and the customer's agreement noted. No re-quote, no ladder: the taxonomy routed it in one step.</p><p><b>Step 4 — submission and the commitments (Chapter 3).</b> Submission reserves the equipment quantities (projected stock at the fulfilling warehouse falls by the order — the procurement lane for the hardware was triggered at conversion, its lead time inside wave one's date), passes the credit check at the customer's limit, and issues the order confirmation. The confirmation pass proves its worth at zero cost: Montaigne AH's operations team confirms the branch sequence against their own rollout plan before anything ships.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the advance (Chapter 6).</b> The advance request issues for the 50% portion; the Payment Entry records receipt against the order; the enterprise book's advance read shows the order funded before the work queue takes it. The advance will allocate against the wave invoices per the schedule as module 7 raises them.</p><p><b>Step 6 — the book carries it (Chapter 7).</b> The order book shows the commitment at value with its three-wave schedule; the weekly planning read carries wave one into the picking horizon; %delivered and %billed sit at zero, ready to advance as module 5 ships wave one. The order file — agreement, evidence, the one amendment, the schedule, the advance — answers the commitment in one lookup, and modules 5 and 7 will write the performance onto it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in Sales Orders, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the promise beside the order.</b> Symptom: commitments made in calls and messages that the order does not state; deliveries and disputes referencing agreements no document carries. Correction: the real agreement amended onto the order with its approvals. Prevention: the standing rule — the order states the whole agreement, and the confirmation puts it in the customer's hands.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the placeholder schedule.</b> Symptom: one optimistic header date on multi-wave reality; overdue flags on rows the customer never expected; planning reads computing from fiction. Correction: the rows re-dated to the real plan by amendment. Prevention: per-row dates stating the agreed schedule at entry.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the amendment side door.</b> Symptom: commercial concessions appearing via amendment without ladder approval; the offer's controls bypassed after acceptance. Correction: the amendments read catches it; the change re-enters the ladder or reverses. Prevention: the change taxonomy — commercial changes re-ladder, always.</p><p><b>Error 4 — reservations held by dead orders.</b> Symptom: stale orders reserving stock for months; projected figures misleading replenishment and availability answers. Correction: the hygiene read — revive with real dates or Close with reason. Prevention: the monthly read and the Closed status used as the decision it is.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the unenforced schedule.</b> Symptom: advance terms on paper, work starting unfunded; milestones passing unbilled. Correction: the advance and milestone reads run; the unbilled portions invoiced. Prevention: the schedule enforced — the work queue gated on the advance where terms say so, and module 7 billing from the schedule.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the silent date slip.</b> Symptom: delivery dates moved in place without reasons or customer agreement; the schedule's history unreadable; service-level conversations conducted from memory. Correction: the movements documented retroactively where evidence allows. Prevention: every date movement an amendment with reason and agreement noted.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the field walk including the customer PO reference, per-row dates, and the schedule table; state submission's four effects and what it does not do; plan waves against stock with the weekly planning read; route changes by the taxonomy through the controlled lanes with the amendments read; operate the milestone schedule with advances requested, received against the order, and allocated at invoicing; and run the order book's six reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 4: the Sales Order states the whole agreement — created from the accepted version or the registers, committing stock and credit at submission, scheduled per row as the real plan, changed only through the taxonomy's lanes with approvals and agreement, funded per its payment schedule, and kept live by the book's reads.</b> Module 5 covers Delivery Notes and returns.</p>"),
]

